Hi, 

I tested and decide speedstep-centrino needs .resume function.

~# echo "1000000" > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_setspeed 
~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq 
1000000
~# cat /proc/cpuinfo
processor       : 0
vendor_id       : GenuineIntel
cpu family      : 6
model           : 9
model name      : Intel(R) Pentium(R) M processor 1100MHz
stepping        : 5
cpu MHz         : 997.561

<<<<SUSPEND and RESUME>>>>>


~# cat /proc/cpuinfo
processor       : 0
vendor_id       : GenuineIntel
cpu family      : 6
model           : 9
model name      : Intel(R) Pentium(R) M processor 1100MHz
stepping        : 5
cpu MHz         : 598.548

~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq 
1000000
~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/cpuinfo_cur_freq 
600000
~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/scaling_cur_freq 
600000

This result tell me that after resume we should call /sys/.../cpuinfo_cur_freq,
in other words, call "cur_freq = cpufreq_get(policy->cpu);" in cpufreq.c.
"cpufreq_get()" is same as "get_cur_freq(unsigned int cpu)" in speedstep-centrino.c.

In my patch, .resume function calls get_cur_freq(0) and set internal 
state variable 'p.state' to proper value.

p.state is only for depricated /proc/acpi/.../performance interface.
so what we need is just calling  get_cur_freq(0).

At Thu, 23 Sep 2004 10:02:07 +0200,
Dominik Brodowski wrote:
> 
> On Thu, Sep 23, 2004 at 02:25:10PM +0900, Hiroshi Miura wrote:
> > Hi,
> > 
> > My note pc, Panasonic Let's Note CF-R3(Pentium M 1.1GHz), troubles when resume.
> > BIOS set cpu p-state as of ac-line state.
> > Centrino driver can not detect change through suspend/resume.
> 
> Actually, it should. Only the (deprecated)
> /proc/acpi/processor/*/performance interface may show a wrong current state,
> /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/{scaling,cpuinfo}_cur_freq should be
> updated by doing a call to ->get().
> 
> Oh, and what suspend method are you using?
> 
